# Reading a null: power, precision, and the Oregon blood-sugar result
subtitle: "How to read a result that is not significant: what Oregon's blood-sugar interval rules out, why the study could hardly have seen a real effect, and what would settle it."
tags: ["statistical power","confidence intervals","null results","medicaid"]
teaches: ["methods/confidence-intervals","methods/statistical-power","methods/randomization"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Genes as instruments: Mendelian randomization","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/genes-as-instruments-mendelian-randomization.cast"}

## Did the bar cross zero?
voice: male
sources:
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker, Taubman, Allen, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Schneider, Wright, Zaslavsky, Finkelstein (Oregon Health Study Group), New England Journal of Medicine 368:1713-22
      year: 2013
      finding: '12,229 people in the Portland area, about 25 months after the 2008 lottery (winning raised Medicaid coverage by 24.1 points). Effects of coverage: glycated haemoglobin of 6.5% or more -0.93 percentage points (95% CI -4.44 to 2.59; control 5.1%); mean glycated haemoglobin +0.01 (-0.09 to 0.11; control 5.3%); diabetes diagnosis +3.83 (1.93 to 5.73); diabetes medication use +5.43 (1.39 to 9.48; control 6.4%); no significant effect on elevated blood pressure or cholesterol. The authors note that medication lowers glycated haemoglobin by about 1 point within 6 months, which with the rise in medication use implies about 0.05 points on the mean, and that their power was limited by the small numbers with these conditions.'
      doi: 10.1056/NEJMsa1212321

