    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["axis","tick_0","tick_1","tick_2","axis_lab","null_line","cite_b","lab_0","ctrl_0","ci_0","study_0","est_0","lo_0","hi_0","verdict","lab_1","ci_1","study_1","est_1","all_gone","all_gone_lab"] hidden true

    card title "Did the bar cross zero?"

If a careful experiment finds no significant effect of health insurance on blood sugar, has it shown that insurance does nothing for blood sugar?
    draw axis tick_0 tick_1 tick_2 axis_lab

That is the question Oregon left behind. In 2008 the state could afford about ten thousand more adults on Medicaid, about ninety thousand sign (@highlight lab_0@)ed up, and it drew names by lottery.
    draw lab_0

About two years later, researchers measured the blood sugar of some tw (@draw null_line@)elve thousand people who had entered the draw.
    draw cite_b

Among those who lost, 5.1 percent had a glycated haemoglobin of 6.5 or more, the usual line for diabetes.
    draw ctrl_0

This dashed line is zero: coverage leaving that share exactly where it was.
    highlight null_line

The estimate, for the people whom winning moved onto Medicaid: 0.93 percentage points fewer above the line.
    draw study_0 est_0

And its ninety-five percent confidence interval runs from 4.44 points fewer to 2.59 points more.
    draw ci_0 lo_0 hi_0

The interval crosses zer (@point at.ref null_line gesture circle@)o, so the result is not significant, and blood pressure and cholesterol came out the same way.

Some read that as a verdict: Medicaid buys people care, but not better physical health.
    draw verdict

To judge that verdict, we will read the interval, then ask how big an effect this study could ever have seen, and then weigh the two readings.
    highlight verdict

    erase verdict

Start with the interval, because two very different results can both fail to be significant.
    draw lab_1

Picture a second study, made up for the comparison, whose interval runs from half a point fewer to 0.3 more.
    draw study_1 ci_1 est_1

    quiz "Both intervals cross zero. Which result tells you more about whether coverage lowers blood sugar?" intro "A quick check."
        right: The narrow one: it rules out every effect bigger than half a point, while Oregon's rules out almost nothing anyone expected.
        wrong: Not significant only says zero is inside. Ask what else each interval leaves inside with it.
        + The narrow made-up one
        * Oregon's wide one
        * Neither: both are not significant

The narrow study has learned something: its data rule out any effect larger than about half a point, either way.
    highlight ci_1

Now hold Oregon's interval against a hard limit: only 5.1 percent were above the line, so no effect can remove more than 5.1 points.
    draw all_gone all_gone_lab

Its left end, at minus 4.44, reaches almost all the way there: coverage clearing nearly nine cases in ten fits these data.
    highlight lo_0 effect circle

And its right end fits a small rise. One interval, compatible with a large gain, with nothing, and with a little harm.
    highlight hi_0 effect circle

So not significant tells you only that zero is inside the interval. Its width tells you how much else is inside with it.
    highlight ci_0 ci_1

## What the average can show
