    card title "What the average can show"

The share above the diabetes line throws away detail, so the study also reported a finer measure: the average glycated haemoglobin itself.
    draw axis tm_0 tm_1 tm_2 tick_0 tick_1 tick_2 axis_lab cite_b

For one person with diabetes, starting medicine lowers it by about one point within six months. That is a large, worthwhile change.
    draw patient patient_lab

Oregon's estimate for the average: plus 0.01 points, with an interval from minus 0.09 to plus 0.11.
    draw est ci est_lab ci_lab

That interval is narrow. It rules out any fall in the average bigger than about a tenth of a point.
    highlight ci

    erase axis_lab

So did coverage fail to do what a single prescription does?
    point at.ref patient gesture circle

Not necessarily. Coverage raised the share (@draw sum_box sum_line_1@) taking diabetes medicine by 5.4 points, from 6.4 percent, with an interval from about 1.4 to 9.5.

And each of those new patients might gain about one point, while everyone else gains nothing, because they had nothing to treat.
    draw sum_line_2

    quiz "If 5.4 people in every 100 start medicine and each drops about one point, how far should the average fall?" intro "Guess first."
        right: About 0.05: one point for 5.4 people in a hundred, spread over all hundred.
        wrong: Most of the hundred take nothing new, and their change is zero. Spread five people's points over all hundred.
        * About one point
        * About half a point
        + About 0.05 points

One point for 5.4 people in a hundred is about 0.05 points on the average. The authors did this same sum themselves.
    draw sum_line_3

    fade tick_1 patient patient_lab to 0

Look closely at the interval.
    camera on ["ci","est_lab","expected_lab","tick_2"]

The fall that medicine alone should have produced, 0.05, sits well inside it.
    draw expected expected_lab

So zero and the expected effect are both inside, and these data cannot tell them apart.
    highlight tick_2 expected

    fade tick_1 patient patient_lab to 1

The narrow interval is narrow about the average, and the average spreads a real gain for a few over many who had nothing to gain.
    camera reset true

    erase sum

One point each for five people in a hundred barely moves the average of all hundred.
    highlight patient patient_lab

Whether we look at the rare outcome with its wide interval, or the average with its narrow one, the same question is left.
    highlight ci est

Could this study have seen the effect it was looking for? That question has a name, power, and it can be worked out from the design, before any result comes in.
    highlight expected

## How big an effect could it see?
