    card title "How big an effect could it see?"

How large would an effect on high blood sugar have to be for a study of Oregon's size to see it reliably?
    draw axes

    hide x_ticks y_ticks

    draw yt_0 yt_2 yt_4 yt_6 yt_8 xt_10 xt_100 parallel true

Start from the interval  (@draw se_box se_line_1@)itself: from minus 4.44 to plus 2.59 is 7.03 points wide.

A ninety-five percent interval is about four standard errors wide, so one standard error is about 1.8 points.
    draw se_line_2

To come out significant four times in five, a true effect must sit about 2.8 standard errors from zero: here, about five points.
    draw se_line_3

This uses only the width of the interval, never the estimate of minus 0.93: power worked out from the estimate itself only restates the p-value.
    highlight se_line_2

And the width was predictable before anyone was measured: a yes-or-no outcome at 5.1 percent, about six thousand people a side, and coverage rising by 0.24 give a standard error near 1.7.
    draw se_line_4 se_line_5

    erase se

Five points. But only 5.1 percent had high blood sugar to begin with, so five points means almost every case gone.
    draw all_line lab_all

That is where Oregon stands: with twelve thousand people, it could reliably see only an effect that cleared nearly every case.
    draw you oregon_lab

That is what low power means: a study that will usually say not significant even when something real is going on.
    highlight you

    quiz "To halve the smallest effect a study can reliably see, how many more people does it need?" intro "Before the curve."
        right: Four times: the error shrinks with the square root of the sample.
        wrong: The error shrinks more slowly than the sample. What halves a square root?
        * Twice as many
        + Four times as many
        * Ten times as many

The standard error falls with the square root of the sample, so this curve drops slowly: four times the people, half the smallest visible effect.
    draw curve_0

Say we wanted to catch a fall of one point, a fifth of all cases. That target is made up fo (@highlight lab_one@)r the exercise, but it is a modest one.
    draw one_line lab_one

Grab the point and move it right, to bigger studies, and watch where the curve comes down to the one-point line.
    explore params ["n"] watch "Watch the point slide right, to bigger studies, until the curve comes down to the one-point line."

It gets there at about three hundred thousand people.
    animate params.n.value 305 duration 4

That is some twenty-five times Oregon's sample, for an effect that would still leave four cases in five.
    draw readout

Part of that cost is the lotte (@highlight oregon_lab@)ry itself: only about one winner in four ended up covered, so the effect of coverage is t (@draw fs_lab@)he winners' gap divided by 0.24, which makes its error about four times larger.

And against a true one-point fall, Oregon's own study wo (@draw power_lab@)uld have come out significant less than one time in ten.

The economist Austin Frakt ran a fuller version of this sum in May 2013 and reached the same verdict: for high blood sugar, the study was many times too small.
    highlight curve_0

A cheaper fix than three hundred thousand people is a better-aimed sample: people who already have diabetes, where a real gain is not spread over nineteen in twenty who were below the line already.
    highlight one_line lab_one

So, for this outcome, not significant was close to guaranteed, whether or not coverage helped, unless it cleared nearly every case.
    highlight oregon_lab power_lab

## Two readings of one table
voice: male
sources:
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker, Taubman, Allen, Bernstein, Gruber, Newhouse, Schneider, Wright, Zaslavsky, Finkelstein (Oregon Health Study Group), New England Journal of Medicine 368:1713-22
      year: 2013
      finding: '12,229 people in the Portland area, about 25 months after the 2008 lottery (winning raised Medicaid coverage by 24.1 points). Effects of coverage: glycated haemoglobin of 6.5% or more -0.93 percentage points (95% CI -4.44 to 2.59; control 5.1%); mean glycated haemoglobin +0.01 (-0.09 to 0.11; control 5.3%); diabetes diagnosis +3.83 (1.93 to 5.73); diabetes medication use +5.43 (1.39 to 9.48; control 6.4%); no significant effect on elevated blood pressure or cholesterol. The authors note that medication lowers glycated haemoglobin by about 1 point within 6 months, which with the rise in medication use implies about 0.05 points on the mean, and that their power was limited by the small numbers with these conditions.'
      doi: 10.1056/NEJMsa1212321
    - id: roy2013
      title: 'Oregon Study: Medicaid ''Had No Significant Effect'' On Health Outcomes vs. Being Uninsured (Forbes)'
      authors: Avik Roy
      year: 2013
      finding: Read the Oregon clinical results as calling into question the roughly $450 billion a year spent on Medicaid.
    - id: frakt2013
      title: Updated power calculation (The Incidental Economist)
      authors: Austin Frakt
      year: 2013
      finding: A power calculation for the Oregon study (May 14, 2013) concluding that it was badly underpowered for the change in the share with elevated glycated haemoglobin.
      url: https://theincidentaleconomist.com/wordpress/updated-power-calculation/
    - id: miller2021
      title: 'Medicaid and Mortality: New Evidence From Linked Survey and Administrative Data'
      authors: Sarah Miller, Norman Johnson, Laura R. Wherry, Quarterly Journal of Economics 136(3):1783-1829
      year: 2021
      finding: Linking federal survey records to death records, ACA Medicaid expansions reduced annual mortality among low-income near-elderly adults (0.132 percentage points, 9.4% of the sample mean).
      doi: 10.1093/qje/qjab004
takeaways:
    - Not significant is not no effect
    - Read the interval, not the star
    - Ask about power first

