    card title "Two readings of one table"

When the table came out in May 2013, why did people read it as proof of opposite things?
    draw axis tick_0 tick_1 tick_2 tick_3 axis_lab null_line cite_b study_0 ci_0 lab_0 est_0

One side read the blood-sugar row as a finding: two years of coverage, no measurable change in physical health.
    highlight ci_0 study_0

That reading had a loud champion: Avik Roy, writi (@draw read_1@)ng in Forbes, argued it called into question the four hundred and fifty billion dollars a year spent on Medicaid.

And the sceptics had a strong point: the narrow interval on the average already rules out a fall of more than a tenth of a point, and blood pressure and cholesterol were flat too.
    highlight est_0

The other side pointed lower down the same table, to what did move. New diabetes diagnoses rose 3.83 points, with an interval from 1.93 to 5.73.
    draw study_1 ci_1 lab_1 est_1

And the use of diabetes medicine rose 5.43 points, from 1.39 to 9.48. Both intervals stay clear of zero.
    draw study_2 ci_2 lab_2 est_2

But those rows show that care changed, not that health did. Only 1.1 percent of the losers had a new diagnosis, so much of the rise is diabetes newly found.
    highlight ci_1 ci_2

The other side saw a chain beginning: coverage (@highlight lab_1@), then a diagnosis, then a pres (@highlight lab_2@)cription, and lower blood sugar as the one lin (@highlight lab_0@)k this study could not resolve.

Austin Frakt and Aaron Carroll, and the aut (@draw read_2@)hors themselves, said the intervals were too wide to rule out changes that would matter clinically.

Neither side misread a number. They disagree about what a wide interval allows you to claim.
    highlight read_1 read_2

What would tell them apart is not a sharper test on this table, but a bigger or better-aimed study: many more people with diabetes, followed for longer, or outcomes that add up over years, such as deaths.
    highlight ci_0

    erase read_1 read_2

Later work took another route. Miller, Johnson and Wherry, in 2021, compared states that did and did not expand Medicaid, and linked survey answers to death records.
    draw mjw

They found fewer deaths among low-income adults aged 55 to 64: a different outcome and a different design, not a verdict on blood sugar.
    highlight mjw

    quiz "A trial of a quit-smoking app reports: quit rate up 0.5 points, ninety-five percent interval from minus 6 to plus 7. What can you say?" intro "One new case."
        right: The trial cannot tell: its interval keeps everything from a six-point harm to a seven-point gain, so it rules out very little either way.
        wrong: Not significant only puts zero inside the interval. Look at what else is inside with it.
        * The app does not work
        * The app helps a little
        + The trial cannot tell: effects from −6 to +7 all fit

So did Oregon show that Medicaid does nothing for blood sugar? No: its interval keeps nothing, and it keeps a large gain.

Read the whole interval, not the star: it holds the effects the data are compatible with, everything outside it is ruled out, and here that is very little.

And ask about power before the result, because an average spread over people who cannot improve hides a real gain for the few who can.

Oregon's blood-sugar row never showed that coverage fails; it showed how large a study must be before its silence means something.
    draw end_ci end_sq end_lo end_hi end_lab

## Where next
